ROLE: novelty_killer  VOTE: yellow

CLOSEST_PRIOR_WORK:
- [{cite: "Wang et al. 2022, E5 (Text Embeddings by Weakly-Supervised Contrastive Pre-training), arXiv:2212.03533", url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.03533", why_close: "Establishes the 'query:'/'passage:' asymmetric prefix mechanism — the exact thing the shipped prompt encodes. The retrieval-gain mechanism is theirs, not the claim's."}]
- [{cite: "Su et al. 2022, INSTRUCTOR / One Embedder, Any Task (TART lineage), arXiv:2212.09741", url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.09741", why_close: "Instruction-conditioned embeddings; same 'prefix the query with model-intended instruction → better retrieval' mechanism. Confirms mechanism is decades-adjacent / well-trodden."}]
- [{cite: "Sentence-Transformers Migration Guide (encode_query/encode_document honor predefined prompts) + config_sentence_transformers.json default_prompt_name semantics", url: "https://www.sbert.net/docs/migration_guide.html", why_close: "The packet's own decisive seam (encode_query applies the prompt, plain encode() with default_prompt_name=null does not) is DOCUMENTED ST behavior, not a discovered mechanism."}]
- [{cite: "MTEB PR#1221 (shipped-prompt support, Aug 2024)", url: "https://github.com/embeddings-benchmark/mteb/pull/1221", why_close: "Shows the ecosystem already recognized that benchmark/integration code must be taught to honor shipped prompts — i.e. the 'integration default doesn't auto-honor' problem is already acknowledged upstream."}]

NAMING_VS_MECHANISM: The novelty is NOT a mechanism delta. The retrieval-gain mechanism (asymmetric query prompt / instruction prefix) is fully attributed to E5/Instructor/TART and the +9.4→+63pp effects are re-derivations of a known effect through a new code path. What is genuinely new is an EMPIRICAL CHARACTERIZATION on the integration-default/packaging axis: the artifact self-describes a prompt, ST's encode_query would honor it, yet the DB wrapper's default __call__ silently routes through plain encode() and drops it — invisibly to a competent user who picked a self-describing model. That is a real, source-verified, multi-wrapper (Chroma/pymilvus/LangChain/LlamaIndex) finding, but it is "known footgun, new (and previously undocumented) silent-default surface," not a new mechanism. This is the boundary between a publishable systems/measurement note and a re-skin.

FATAL_COLLISIONS: None that KILL. The decisive test (plain .encode() applies NO prompt because default_prompt_name=null → seam is at the wrapper boundary, not inside ST) is the correct, falsifying check and it cleared the costume concern in ≥2 verifiable sources (ST config semantics + reproduced BEIR numbers). No single prior work both (a) names the model-self-describes-prompt → DB-wrapper-default-silently-drops-it seam AND (b) measures it across wrappers. So no green-killing collision exists. But the absence of a kill is NOT mechanism novelty.

MANDATORY_RELATED_WORK:
- E5 / Wang 2022 (arXiv:2212.03533) — mechanism attribution; MUST cite so the contribution is not mis-stated as a new mechanism.
- INSTRUCTOR / TART (arXiv:2212.09741) — instruction-conditioned embedding prior art.
- Sentence-Transformers Migration Guide + config_sentence_transformers.json prompt/default_prompt_name semantics — the decisive seam is documented ST behavior and must be cited as such.
- MTEB PR#1221 (shipped-prompt support) — prior ecosystem recognition of the "integration must honor shipped prompts" problem; failing to cite would overstate novelty.
- The four wrapper sources (Chroma SentenceTransformerEmbeddingFunction __call__/embed_query; pymilvus query_instruction; LangChain query_encode_kwargs; LlamaIndex query_instruction) — these ARE the contribution's evidentiary core and must be cited at the version pinned.

RATIONALE: Per role mandate I separate naming from mechanism. The mechanism is decades-known and correctly cited (E5/Wang2022; Instructor/TART), so there is no mechanism delta — every A<B number is a re-measurement of a known effect. The decisive test was run correctly and resolves the costume risk (default_prompt_name=null ⇒ plain encode() applies no prompt ⇒ seam is at the wrapper boundary, confirmed in ST docs + reproduced SciFact numbers), and no prior work collides on the *specific* "self-describing model → DB wrapper default silently drops the shipped prompt → invisible to a competent user" framing, so RED is unwarranted (no verified ≥2-source collision). But MTEB PR#1221 plus the documented ST migration semantics show the underlying "integration code must be taught to honor shipped prompts" problem is already known to the ecosystem; the claim's contribution is the previously-undocumented silent-default surface across four production DB wrappers — an integration/packaging measurement, not a mechanism. That is real and worth publishing as a systems/footgun characterization, but it sits below the GREEN bar for novelty: the delta is empirical/packaging, not mechanistic. Honest vote: YELLOW.
